<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Innviertel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Innviertel is a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> between the Inn, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the Salzach rivers, with towns such as Braunau am Inn and Ried. Part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> for centuries, it was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> in 1779. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> returned it to Bavaria in 1810, but the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> of 1816 gave it back to Austria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/innviertel.jpg" alt="Innviertel" width="300" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: plp · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Aut_Ooe_Innviertel.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Innviertel, officially the Innkreis, is the north-western of the four traditional quarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, the others being the Hausruckviertel, Mühlviertel and Traunviertel. It includes the districts of Braunau am Inn, Ried im Innkreis and Schärding and covers about 2,250 square kilometres. The landscape is a fertile, flat to hilly part of the Alpine foothills between the Salzach, Inn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the Hausruck hills. Its main towns are Braunau am Inn, Ried im Innkreis and Schärding, which is known as a tourist centre for its baroque town centre. Since the formation of political districts in 1868 the quarters have had no legal basis and are purely regional names.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the sixth century most of the Innviertel was part of the Mattiggau region of the stem duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, with ducal courts at Ranshofen and Mattighofen. In 1507 it became part of the Rentamt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghausen">Burghausen</a>. For centuries the area served Bavaria as an eastern defence line against the rising Archduchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and as arable land. During the Bavarian uprising of 1705 to 1706 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> occupation, Braunau was the seat of the short-lived Bavarian State Defence Congress in December 1705, called the Braunau Parliament.</p><p>The death in 1777 of the childless Bavarian elector Maximilian III Joseph, which ended the Bavarian line of the Wittelsbachs, led to the War of the Bavarian Succession. With the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> the Innviertel became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> in 1779. At first it was called the Fünfte Viertel, the Fifth Quarter, and before that it had been known as Innbaiern. The population was not content under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, and the cry Lieber bayrisch sterben als kaiserlich verderben, better to die Bavarian than suffer imperial ruin, was long in circulation. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> of 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> took the Innviertel again in 1810. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 1 May 1816.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The people of the Innviertel kept their Bavarian dialect, a West Central Austro-Bavarian variety, despite adopting much Austrian standard vocabulary. The colourful house facades of the Inn-Salzach style in its towns recall Bavarian tradition. Rivalry with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a> lasted into the twentieth century, and today the inhabitants identify more closely with their region than those of the other quarters. Franz Xaver Gruber, composer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silent-night">Silent Night</a>, the Schwanthaler family of sculptors, the conscientious objector Franz Jägerstätter and the physicist Anton Zeilinger were born there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Innviertel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As a member of the Confederation of the Rhine from 1806, Bavaria absorbed Nuremberg, Ansbach, and in 1810 it gained Bayreuth, Regensburg, Salzburg and the <b>Innviertel</b>, bringing it to its greatest extent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
